#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096402 :question/id #uuid "2c37ae73-233d-5e0d-87ff-05cacc6d078b" :question/math-academy-id "e-183" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-3x}},$ calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x)) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{g}} = g^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x)^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2(1-3x)^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096496 :question/id #uuid "e8b9014e-32cf-5f04-856c-7fd663bea50f" :question/math-academy-id "e-528" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $f'(x) - 3\\sin(2x) = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 3\\sin{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096497 :question/id #uuid "a8e6c58c-da62-5dab-8325-a53c14d9b8ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-529" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} = x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+Cx+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$ We cannot combine them, because $C$ is being multiplied by $x$ while $D$ is not."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096498 :question/id #uuid "ab40a4d3-c418-5b64-a04c-bb017155a4d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-530" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $y'  = 2x,$ and sketch the solution curves of this equation." :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\ny = x^2+C.\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,1.$\n\n![](images/e3/e37771799ef5fadc259e6ea0073042a83e0974b5a2619d1af3e87a7467e6d780.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096499 :question/id #uuid "29a0265e-ad88-5ac9-88fe-878aa4f0d0bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-531" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation below that satisfies the condition $f(0)=2.$\n\n$$\nf'(x) - \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2} = 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x) = 3\\arctan{x} + C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(0) = 2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C \\\\ 2 & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is \n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\arctan x + 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd726c27-0178-5cc8-a8b6-82e60d62ac1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(15x^{4} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093807} {:answer/id #uuid "c477f2d6-5ad8-52b3-8fe5-92a70939ea8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(5x^{5} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093808} {:answer/id #uuid "060329dc-443f-5ee6-a297-ff049fb57b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8(15x^{4} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093809} {:answer/id #uuid "113fc8bd-4460-5e48-a12a-43e5a61ddd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(15x^{4} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093810} {:answer/id #uuid "5927f4b1-57be-52eb-ac32-2223134b64df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(5x^{4} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093811}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9edef6e7-9845-5317-bcbd-d2c7b7bf754e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093806}] :question/id #uuid "d119ffe6-ff07-57d9-a572-ef180eb72f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-11414" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{3}$, find $f^{′}(x)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2cdeff7-1bd1-52cc-b4d2-58765a1e715e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = - (1)/(2(x - 1)) + \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094094} {:answer/id #uuid "e264582e-6aa3-5da8-b05d-657aca25a556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{x - 1} + \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094095} {:answer/id #uuid "43d2def0-e7d1-54fb-834f-84b6876ea10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{x - 1} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094096} {:answer/id #uuid "518e0850-647a-5199-8686-13cfd746ca8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{x - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094097} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc7592f-e41a-5c1f-9369-d6bc76a6ea98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{x - 1} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094098}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9dd411f0-cb37-5900-866e-8862a9b0a1e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094093}] :question/id #uuid "d93ad997-2d1a-586a-bd5a-d470614a6ec6" :question/math-academy-id "q-119078" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(x - 1)^{2}y^{′} =-2$ that satisfies the condition $y(2) = 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f651d57-fd13-5223-b101-19044f5a3b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x) =-\\frac{11}{2}x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094682} {:answer/id #uuid "0c189129-a43b-5083-8426-a8d988b5bcfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x) = \\frac{11}{2} - C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094683} {:answer/id #uuid "441b0a08-b772-5959-b466-9ce911e9e6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x) =-\\frac{11}{2}x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094684} {:answer/id #uuid "63128db0-6242-55ce-a386-75928939d9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x) = \\frac{11}{2}x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094685} {:answer/id #uuid "7833225a-e7d7-58d8-a243-47f76b32d976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x) = 11x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094686}] :answer-field/id #uuid "702c2b7a-1035-57b4-98fc-9ee1ec455ef0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094681}] :question/id #uuid "e1c8faa6-71a3-52b0-9c4c-3e41ed01dd83" :question/math-academy-id "q-14245" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $f^{′}(x) + 11x = 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85752216-72d1-52c5-a307-1f7593bb2baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095425} {:answer/id #uuid "bffd2b2f-d555-5e17-8756-8a754febf526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095426} {:answer/id #uuid "eef892e3-6947-592f-9345-311b595e0536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(4x^{3})(x^{4} + 3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095427} {:answer/id #uuid "05327ffd-b783-558f-8116-edad45453171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(4x^{3})(x^{4} + 3x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095428} {:answer/id #uuid "62baa9f7-a4b9-5ff4-84f6-37fdf189f3fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095429}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a72fa786-3004-51a5-a233-17a9a216dfb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095424}] :question/id #uuid "b551e489-4c37-53ea-831b-8f3ca007450b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48625" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{df}{dx}$ when $f(x) = (x^{4} + 3x)^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256d8104-e561-50d4-bb47-c84cda5f979d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x-1{)}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327259} {:answer/id #uuid "92909c43-4d9f-55c2-9367-b2903d4d10f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328210} {:answer/id #uuid "b84fdb58-69a3-5339-b857-edeff32c5507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328539} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdb8149-860b-5213-8db1-d92c7a922a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328733} {:answer/id #uuid "e32be03e-6bf9-5893-a620-899e6e49abe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(5x-1{)}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327259} :answer-field/id #uuid "14eebaf9-3e88-5f2b-82c3-330efcb42e3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "854ec833-4bc0-54f0-9210-c708a9342fef" :question/math-academy-id "q-48730" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5x-1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x-1.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x-1{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d468059-f4b7-51ec-a214-229418cddeaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4x - 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095473} {:answer/id #uuid "ce172954-02de-5948-98e6-063d373c04b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x - 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095474} {:answer/id #uuid "dd38fa2b-33de-5df5-aa85-ed357c5eb5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4x + 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095475} {:answer/id #uuid "fd120f87-d6a9-56e7-ae4f-db302afd2580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x + 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095476} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7778bb-8979-5802-bf93-4a409e6e58b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4x - 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095477}] :answer-field/id #uuid "84ab9969-a822-5aa8-beea-30e48f66c60f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095472}] :question/id #uuid "ea97309c-a67a-5e7f-aa97-023276ac6669" :question/math-academy-id "q-48846" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{4x - 3}$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17e908ac-775f-57c5-b411-f2580198a03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(8x+6)}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524427} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8e41b2-c271-53b2-95cd-a26907947e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+3}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524499} {:answer/id #uuid "b770c35b-70a6-5c86-ad4d-dd5c5297261a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4x+3)}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524674} {:answer/id #uuid "f1844554-3506-5984-a850-e337e7d94eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4x+3)}{\\sqrt[3]{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524807} {:answer/id #uuid "f8192899-3a5d-5635-a01d-e3e96a9ce1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4x+3)}{2\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524674} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd41183-7068-5455-b0e0-8cdceb8831b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2da730c2-fcbd-535d-85d1-2014785f31eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48850" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+3x$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{g}}=2{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =2({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{′}=4x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (4x+3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x+3}{{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x+3}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9acd2481-0ec3-5994-9168-964cb3c474c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x) = \\frac{1}{2}e^{x} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095665} {:answer/id #uuid "b3631b1e-e008-5fb9-a825-84c8ecba10b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x) = e^{x} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095666} {:answer/id #uuid "c508b657-4c25-56c5-960c-217135f76315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x) =-\\frac{1}{2}e^{x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095667} {:answer/id #uuid "f83fba29-574c-5d24-b1a6-4adbe9283fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x) = \\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095668} {:answer/id #uuid "38b83252-dae4-5abd-841f-1818a0da90fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x) =-\\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095669}] :answer-field/id #uuid "62dcfac4-1b46-5254-bc04-921bedd65cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095664}] :question/id #uuid "36f54028-9879-56b6-bec9-c97cd80fb58e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49942" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation below that satisfies the condition $f(0) =-1$.\n\n$2f^{′}(x) + e^{x} = 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a181c2-eaa7-54a0-8890-e935b4d4ff1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{2x}-2{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330015} {:answer/id #uuid "15c676e1-03b3-5352-b3b8-1386a7ac7b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330129} {:answer/id #uuid "c78be5e7-440c-5080-aede-7717891b5955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331715} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb467dd-d851-57e6-ba23-4d6c40542276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}-2{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331773} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0ac1c2-8887-5016-9ea9-5bc26155b0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-4{e}^{2x}-2{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330129} :answer-field/id #uuid "7491e487-3ec6-578a-921d-573211c3741a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb82aa2f-3e4a-523a-94c2-c95aa3cb8ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-50136" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4{e}^{2x}=-2x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{2x}-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}y & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89aee0c2-41c4-50d9-95e7-d07999f8e43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 5x^{2} + Cx + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095725} {:answer/id #uuid "a8264bab-88eb-5c1b-8cf6-4b5658e50c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = Cx^{2} + 10x + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095726} {:answer/id #uuid "c8066636-9a63-5ade-85aa-07f7e0be5cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y =-5x^{2} + Cx + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095727} {:answer/id #uuid "0a099fae-b25d-5abb-a50a-c5dc1eda7e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y =-5x^{2} + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095728} {:answer/id #uuid "1a40f9c2-33a9-5233-bec8-c51acd272979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y =-5x^{2} + Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095729}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8c860608-75a8-5451-a1a7-a0683cdbc84f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a4235d-41c9-5a0e-b658-3ec6b027584e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{2}+Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398891} {:answer/id #uuid "4b317621-35b4-53d1-939d-fab67d8f1031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398892} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcf3415-556b-53c7-ac6f-21a5ddefd845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{2}+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398893} {:answer/id #uuid "a552a9bb-4de8-5448-90ec-372d3554e55d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398894} {:answer/id #uuid "fa85c5fe-d852-5230-88fd-53d91e4bcd53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{2}+10x+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398895}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398892} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1789600-f409-5b68-8f83-26b29073f519" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "17751a3b-ed4b-5966-a450-330460c02715" :question/math-academy-id "q-50139" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=-10.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-10\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-10x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-10x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-10\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D \\\\  & =-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a745d53-2adc-5935-a97d-5a586599fa37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{3}e^{3x} + Cx + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095731} {:answer/id #uuid "f4387418-5075-52c7-b685-924f2791a4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{9}e^{3x} + Cx^{2} + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095732} {:answer/id #uuid "035eddbc-75fb-53ba-bc61-c5e0c4f3f025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{9}e^{3x} + Cx + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095733} {:answer/id #uuid "12887777-c8a6-5e16-bf30-ed290559c1e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{9}e^{3x} + Cx^{2} + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095734} {:answer/id #uuid "7a68fbfe-8f1c-512a-8cc2-1b00fe18af49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{3}e^{3x} + Cx + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095735}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5a4615-20d2-563a-8738-3f3905aa1549" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095730} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae67e9eb-7a6a-5f1f-9633-a869be11af9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398897} {:answer/id #uuid "1e9d1539-56f7-56d2-9de6-1e9901343c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398898} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9ac46a-1dd6-5423-b497-fd6f6d213a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398899} {:answer/id #uuid "bd19773a-598c-5d4a-9935-ec0892a47ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398900} {:answer/id #uuid "89be7220-c09b-594d-8bff-c3a06f0b9221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398899} :answer-field/id #uuid "30eeac9f-d833-5bc8-934f-4097337092f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e04bc45-f34f-56fc-9a0e-8643597c4852" :question/math-academy-id "q-50148" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\,{e}^{3x})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "853aeea8-65f0-5fb9-8258-80667aea2abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8b/8bd666d1415f83ff8232e39963248ef0afe62f39e66d5f3416ae18316453faa3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095737} {:answer/id #uuid "a94dc35e-7f42-58d5-bacb-d6ebe1a5ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0a/0ab0b12088ac6816f7086e952e749ea6d4c9107cc6189ebb0efcafb904152ad0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095738} {:answer/id #uuid "efb18e51-9c8d-5683-8dc6-f9dd9a0e7082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1a/1a0e6d6d025e2f8b79019036637efd78b89954d1b01b00037ece77df4c3844eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095739} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9b1208-989e-52fe-9319-003ca0fe7916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/65/65ae1770ff4084d5a89fdda0f552f2a92380ae948c2b0f1ef3d3bb5d20f32713.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095740} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3f4a30-dc1d-5668-a728-9b3bc043b41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5d/5d686380cd025e83e38524918c5cfaf215726f0f0d05783b8f88fd5f281c091c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095741}] :answer-field/id #uuid "baa5bcf1-a2e1-5611-80a8-e28d46985c9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095736} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e8c57d-66b6-54a2-a409-f04037ac2dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8b/8bd666d1415f83ff8232e39963248ef0afe62f39e66d5f3416ae18316453faa3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392710} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7c9c8c-a18a-575b-bbc5-e03ae2420c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5d/5d686380cd025e83e38524918c5cfaf215726f0f0d05783b8f88fd5f281c091c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392711} {:answer/id #uuid "f007b1ae-80cd-5a65-860b-3cf4ed12c156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0a/0ab0b12088ac6816f7086e952e749ea6d4c9107cc6189ebb0efcafb904152ad0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392712} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ff690e-b86c-57a2-9518-abe046479e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/65/65ae1770ff4084d5a89fdda0f552f2a92380ae948c2b0f1ef3d3bb5d20f32713.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392713} {:answer/id #uuid "06d4b073-7af6-56cc-939c-5a4f1e989fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1a/1a0e6d6d025e2f8b79019036637efd78b89954d1b01b00037ece77df4c3844eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392712} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7f2c5a-e3a6-5cfd-9077-9d13a03ddc70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a33d5262-3afa-580b-b512-aba7c2576e69" :question/math-academy-id "q-50195" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int (x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =2\\int (x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](images/92/92a2dbaa5726e6a1ab83fa879ad7b630b05e68a8e366db214b74d85269a849a9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b595fdc-ac24-53b1-b485-072a76711f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/83/83c166a9882661ec123b3f14bf76c9c7585052d0e85e24c4e41b7bbde4de9326.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330631} {:answer/id #uuid "71cb7ed8-c659-56d9-95c6-9bd73867660f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1e/1e746142e1dc26c247a6ac985c65072445658ad1f9b5ae298d246c1d81ab96bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330973} {:answer/id #uuid "7a615d72-2639-5796-9876-984ecf702dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/56/566935c224696f817a798a9e0f608ba384b5da6af1f0bfec6ad769e910f1230c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331033} {:answer/id #uuid "8feb98b8-4798-558b-9d2a-1181de6e3f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4b/4bcb4f24caba3010cb8bfffecacbd392ad5951d4e42873d467c9007ad2b7ea46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331227} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f5ab31-edff-5dee-88f0-7ee4a29422a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1f/1fd9c2f2358171f102969930fde05347f50bd497f725fdc4c24fec97490f15ad.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330631} :answer-field/id #uuid "f42fe6aa-d5ce-5802-8ca2-328c4a548b5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "27b042e7-c453-5f49-bac9-de5985bbacde" :question/math-academy-id "q-50196" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3(x+1)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-3\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](images/03/03a5bf5134dc7d551b2602145c4c25081a2ad274f02c95845e5789b288e618a1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2e9e80-9009-5c4d-8418-53807b048413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333097} {:answer/id #uuid "2f451149-34e3-5874-ac22-67e5f7f806cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18x(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333429} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1f9842-ca0a-55db-bcb6-06735b728dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(4+6x{)}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334496} {:answer/id #uuid "b1df2cc8-3e8e-59c7-a92f-f4f573b06622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334551} {:answer/id #uuid "d848947b-033e-583b-9707-793c5c4bf6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30(4+6x{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333097} :answer-field/id #uuid "564e943b-61e3-5dd9-b71b-ad37ff586952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f0bd56a8-5c99-5166-be8e-4185bfb62ed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-666" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(4+6x{)}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{3},\\,g=4+6x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3{g}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\cdot 6\\cdot (4+6x{)}^{2} \\\\  & =18(4+6x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100728 :question/id #uuid "dbb4e0a0-55ec-5cc7-9617-99cec8108940" :question/math-academy-id "e-540" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y' = 5y$ that satisfies $y(0)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =5x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm {e}^{5x+C} \\\\  & =\\pm {e}^{C}{e}^{5x} \\\\  & =K{e}^{5x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm e^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we apply the condition $y(0) = 3$ to find $K\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3 \\\\ K{e}^{5\\cdot 0} & =3 \\\\ K & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $y' = 5y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=3$ is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e^{5x}  \\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100732 :question/id #uuid "891e9004-961b-539a-ab93-0752347fd077" :question/math-academy-id "e-541" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\cos(3y)\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x) = 0$ given that  $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0.$ Assume that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} < y < \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} .$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1=3C.$\n\nNow let's apply the condition $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0$ to find $C_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =0+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\sin(3y)   =  \\dfrac {3} {2} \\cos(2x)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ad7b38-011b-5bbc-ad6f-119764a28f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284794} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd60163-91c2-5f68-b12b-44f98e6c673c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4x}+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285640} {:answer/id #uuid "60978d4b-4f9a-549a-a78c-7b2ab10d8ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285674} {:answer/id #uuid "675c44fb-e5b5-5cc4-a665-61ee54152b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}+4{e}^{5}-1}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285732} {:answer/id #uuid "dd44c23d-4f4e-5432-b33e-dff52c07b119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+{e}^{5}+\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285732} :answer-field/id #uuid "af959d06-569d-5cc3-8e3f-fadee181c763" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e844aafc-6198-5e85-8b96-36f86924e412" :question/math-academy-id "q-20071" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x-y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{4x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4\\cdot 0}}{4}+C) & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}+C) & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+C & ={e}^{5} \\\\ C & =\\frac{4{e}^{5}-1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=5$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}+4{e}^{5}-1}{4}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "446c4857-dd0c-5efd-afb0-2ea4ed0660a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324526} {:answer/id #uuid "748ec2ec-3297-5615-86ab-bc1f21ac26f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324942} {:answer/id #uuid "9c45be7d-d1cc-59a4-8002-8c72c910bd2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325297} {:answer/id #uuid "d5becbca-f53a-5866-bb37-73887a660a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325829} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e878f5-c14b-5673-a208-7eea879de351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325297} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6850fb7-3a0b-5d41-af17-c643ce765bf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6c894685-37ff-5d18-b9c8-6edb0d037176" :question/math-academy-id "q-4019" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}\n$$ that satisfies $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3} & ={x}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =3{x}^{2}+3{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}$  is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{13})}^{3} & =3(2)^{2}+C \\\\ 13 & =12+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}$ is\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289b6d64-a211-5da5-9a4e-e626db7e48e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4{e}^{x}}{x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345715} {:answer/id #uuid "539c1b15-7731-56bd-9c2a-ca623649b96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{e}^{x+1}}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346061} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3ba5d5-d097-5032-a55f-f8306cff701c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346163} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfbb45a-ec8c-5ace-9f06-4292bd88e210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346719} {:answer/id #uuid "adf0d1d2-3ce8-5813-906f-ee26fca0599b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346163} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1ace9b-199b-55d1-97e0-bde3eb07a2d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "167e1aa1-119e-56a2-b6bf-0adbf44240f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79372" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1\n$$ that satisfies $y(1)=e.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\left(\\frac{1+x}{x}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (1-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =\\frac{K{e}^{x}}{x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=e$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =e \\\\ \\frac{K{e}^{1}}{1+1} & =e \\\\ K & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=e$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{x+1}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "531c9876-41a6-5528-8716-512d9860a650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349207} {:answer/id #uuid "63de5349-a542-5177-bb56-e3b3bd658803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349360} {:answer/id #uuid "86d94f8e-237b-5f46-bbef-54ff8f75c5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349675} {:answer/id #uuid "89e3ffe6-f402-5afd-82f1-aa385f26d411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349698} {:answer/id #uuid "d835efde-95ab-5abd-b768-7494d10b73eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349360} :answer-field/id #uuid "375659ba-c2ed-5aba-9b69-1aebcdb68de1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "525ed303-6429-5295-90a0-f24212406b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-8310" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$ that satisfies $y(1)=2.$ Assume that $x>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming $x>0,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+C} & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{C} & =2 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=2$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{-1}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2})}=\\frac{2}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124370 :question/id #uuid "a568fc63-e81c-5210-93f0-bcbd429a9e40" :question/math-academy-id "e-6678" :question/problem "A biologist observed that the growth of the number of fish in a lake is directly proportional to the difference between the lake’s maximum fish population and the current population. Let $P(t)$ be the fish population, measured in number of fish, and let $t \\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum fish population that the lake can support is $200$ fish, which differential equation could be used to model the lake’s fish population? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the fish population $P(t)$ of the lake changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum fish population the lake can support and the current fish population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(a - P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum fish population the lake can support, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 200 \\textrm{ fish},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(200 - P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124371 :question/id #uuid "d603efdd-6e27-5e14-a106-f9fc4a182403" :question/math-academy-id "e-6679" :question/problem "In an aquarium, there are initially $20$ fish. After $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50.$ Assuming inhibited growth, calculate the number of fish after $100$ weeks if the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $90$ fish." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of fish $N(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total number of fish and the current number of fish. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k\\left(a-N\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 90$ fish, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(90-N).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dN}{90-N} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dN}{90-N} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90-N| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $90 - N > 0$ for all $t \\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $N(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90-N & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 90-N & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 90-N & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ N & =90-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial number of fish was $20.$ So, we apply the initial condition $N(0)=20,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =20 \\\\ 90-K{e}^{0} & =20 \\\\ 90-K & =20 \\\\ K & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $N(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nN(t)=90 - 70 e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50.$ So, applying the condition $N(30)=50,$ we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(30) & =50 \\\\ 90-70{e}^{-30k} & =50 \\\\ 40 & =70{e}^{-30k} \\\\ {e}^{-30k} & =\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ -30k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nN(t)=90-70e^{(t/30)\\,\\ln\\left(4/7\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally,  the number of fish after $t=100$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(100) & =90-70{e}^{(100/30)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4/7)} \\\\  & =79.161… \\\\  & ≈79\\,\\text{fish},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124373 :question/id #uuid "d178c83a-b8af-5d6a-bf40-fcfb7506feeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6686" :question/problem "Given that $y \\lt 60$ and $t \\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?\n\nI. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y - 240$\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y^2 - 240$\n\nIV. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4 - 240y$" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ for all $t \\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4(60-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and $a=60.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y \\lt 60$ for all $t \\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c41e7d9-f7c3-59f1-82a1-92abfdfa62ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.5(150-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445340} {:answer/id #uuid "4611a9e7-a8cc-5133-9e08-255ac5b7a2fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.5(y-150)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445343} {:answer/id #uuid "5853d9b3-2ca6-58cf-bf5c-4c5d47d3fa61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=150(y+0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445344} {:answer/id #uuid "7e94fce8-561e-540e-9a23-0e72db5154a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-0.5(y+150)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445347} {:answer/id #uuid "fa740537-bee8-5451-a7ef-49020d73940b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=150(y-0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445340} :answer-field/id #uuid "79311994-ead5-5028-b4a4-de97196cfb35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3ff73a32-c10e-5c6a-a056-14d519235f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-106661" :question/problem "Given that $y<150$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.5(150-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.5,$ and $a=150.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<150$ for all $t\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd4d9520-8eb8-590a-8484-786df4886f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5-400y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397851} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2d8367-f11c-56cb-b66f-27a8c632bc41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y-400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397852} {:answer/id #uuid "906da5cb-d9e8-5ec3-b4fd-cd8abe7cdb40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397853} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1db174-5614-5103-8d7e-cb38f5042fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5{y}^{2}-400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397854} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c36cb8-43dc-59ec-b220-e1d3bce29295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397855}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397853} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3476a6-1afc-5395-a095-cd169e19c687" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "092a8c01-7fc1-532c-ba20-9cb4c823a717" :question/math-academy-id "q-106662" :question/problem "Given that $y<80$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5(80-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=80.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<80$ for all $t\\ge 0.$"}]}
